0
6000
4500
```

### When should you use interval DP in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the risk computation engine multiplies a chain of covariance and factor matrices for portfolio risk. The dimensions vary widely because some matrices represent individual securities and others represent market factors. The engine runs the matrix chain DP to find the cheapest multiplication order before executing the actual matrix operations, saving orders of magnitude in floating-point work on large portfolios.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RiskMatrixOptimiser {

    public static int optimiseChain(int[] factorDimensions) {
        int n = factorDimensions.length - 1;
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int len = 2; len <= n; len++)
            for (int i = 0; i <= n - len; i++) {
                int j = i + len - 1;
                dp[i][j] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
                for (int k = i; k < j; k++)
                    dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j],
                        dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j]
                        + factorDimensions[i] * factorDimensions[k+1] * factorDimensions[j+1]);
            }
        return dp[0][n - 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] dims = {100, 20, 50, 10, 80};
        System.out.println("Minimum scalar multiplications: " + optimiseChain(dims));
    }
}
```

```text
Minimum scalar multiplications: 220000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that matrix chain multiplication is the archetypal interval DP problem. The pattern of choosing a split point within a range and combining the costs of the two halves appears in burst balloons, minimum cost to cut a stick, and optimal binary search tree.

The number of parenthesisations follows the Catalan numbers, C(n) = (2n)! / ((n+1)! * n!), which grows exponentially. The cubic DP is a massive improvement over exhaustive enumeration.

Knuth's optimisation can sometimes reduce the cubic time by constraining the range of k values, but the standard O(n^3) version is what interviewers expect.
